<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  DEBUG-ONLY manifest. Merged into debug builds only; the release APK never sees it.

  INTERNET is required in debug because the Dart VM service and the Compose tooling
  inspector both open a socket back to the development host; without it the IDE cannot
  attach and hot reload does not work.

  scripts/check_offline.sh runs aapt against build/app/outputs/apk/release/app-release.apk
  and fails if android.permission.INTERNET appears there. Because Gradle merges per
  source-set, its presence here is exactly what makes that check meaningful: the same
  dependency graph produces an APK with the permission in debug and without it in
  release. If it ever appears in release, something stopped being debug-only.

  THE MICROPHONE PERMISSIONS BELOW ARE DEBUG-ONLY FOR THE SAME REASON, and this is the more
  important half of the file. NeuralEngineFactory.USE_REAL_NEURAL is `false`, so no shipped
  build of this app contains a working speech recogniser; it would have been trivially easy to
  declare RECORD_AUDIO in the main manifest "for later" and never think about it again. But a
  release APK that advertises microphone access it never uses is a permission prompt in
  disguise, and this project's entire claim is that it asks for nothing it does not use.
  Declaring it here means a user installing the release is shown no microphone prompt and the
  OS grants nothing.

  If the speech engine is ever enabled, meaning a real ONNX model, a real runtime, and
  USE_REAL_NEURAL flipped to true, then these four move back to the main manifest. That is
  deliberate extra work, because doing it should require a conscious decision that the feature
  now exists, not a side effect of writing the code.

  UPDATE 2026-10-04: that decision was made. The four permissions are back in
  app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml, and this file is once again only about INTERNET.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
</manifest>
